/**
 * @page usb_classes USB Classes
 *
 * A **device class** is a ready-made, host-recognised function: it owns the
 * descriptors and the class protocol, so your code supplies only behaviour through
 * callbacks. Every class follows the same three steps - usbip_device_create() a
 * device, `*_add()` the class, usbip_device_plug() it. Writing your own class uses
 * the same public API (see @ref usb_class "the class layer").
 *
 * Class      | Header                | Reference
 * ---------- | --------------------- | ---------------
 * HID        | `classes/hid.h`       | @ref class_hid
 * CDC-ACM    | `classes/cdc_acm.h`   | @ref class_cdc
 * MSC        | `classes/msc.h`       | @ref class_msc
 * MTP        | `classes/mtp.h`       | @ref class_mtp
 * UAC        | `classes/uac.h`       | @ref class_uac
 * UVC        | `classes/uvc.h`       | @ref class_uvc
 * DFU        | `classes/dfu.h`       | @ref class_dfu
 * Bluetooth  | `classes/bluetooth.h` | @ref class_bt
 *
 * Each reference page documents the class's `*_opts` struct, its entry point and
 * every callback it can hand you. Every class but Bluetooth also has a runnable
 * program under `examples/device/`, and @ref cl_linux / @ref cl_windows tabulate what
 * each class turns into once a host imports it.
 *
 * Not everything ships as a class: @ref dev_vendor covers vendor-specific and WebUSB
 * devices built on the core alone, and
 * @ref dev_composite covers putting several functions on one device.
 */
